
Global Automotive Thermal Cutoff market size was valued at USD 43.84 million in 2025. The market is projected to grow from USD 45.20 million in 2026 to USD 76.05 million by 2034, exhibiting a CAGR of 8.2% during the forecast period.
Automotive thermal cutoff is a one‑shot, non‑resettable over‑temperature protection device deployed in automotive electronics, motors, heaters and auxiliary circuits. When the preset temperature is reached the device opens the circuit, preventing overheating, thermal runaway and fire risk. The technology family comprises organic‑type OTCO and alloy‑type ATCO configurations.
Growth reflects expanding vehicle electrification, stricter safety regulations and rising demand for reliable thermal management solutions across passenger and commercial vehicles. Production reached roughly 800 million units in 2025 with an average price of about USD 60 per thousand units. Leading manufacturers such as Schott, Emerson, UCHIHASHI and Microtherm continue to invest in material innovations and automated assembly lines to meet OEM requirements.
